 experience like that probably does lead  to an exceptional response and that's  what we're now grappling with the  aftermath of kovid but also we are  continuing to invest in public services  and i think you know that's what your  viewers and people want us to to be able  to do as well particularly and most  importantly the nhs and social care  getting on with tackling the backlog  there pulling more police officers on  the street putting more money into our  classrooms those are all the things the  government is doing we're seeing very  strong investment in public services  over this parliament  and that of course needs to be paid for  but but there are those who will say  that what you did yesterday  didn't go far enough and didn't actually  help the very poorest in society there's  no increase in universal credit there's  no increase in pensions no increase in  disability benefit  the very poorest in society do not earn  enough to benefit from the announcements  that you made yesterday do they they are  not going to benefit from an increased  national insurance  threshold or a one percent tax cut  because they just simply don't earn  enough and for those people there was  nothing yesterday  well they actually are my priority which  is why we started our action to help  them in the autumn in my autumn budget  we made the operation of universal  credit more generous so that people can  keep more of what they are and  especially those people that you're  talking about on low incomes they will  also benefit and that what we did then  was a two billion pound tax cut for  about two million low earners they will  also disproportionately benefit from the  significant increase in the national  living wage that's just about to come in  it's going to go up 6.6  now the combination of all these things  means that for example let's take a  single mother with two children renting  working full time on the national living  wage so a low earner as you described  that that mother is going to be  1600 pounds a year better off as a  result of all these decisions and it's  right that we are focusing attention on  them but i do believe the policies that  we're putting in place will help those  people on low incomes disproportionately  in fact that's what you believe but but  it's not what other people who've  examined what you've you've set out  yesterday believe you know the  resolution foundation says that  two-thirds of the benefits you announced  yesterday will will help only the top 50  tax cuts for middle and top earners only  one in three of the pounds that is going  to be saved goes to the least well-off  and you look at what you've  you've really flagged up to us  today about fuel duty going down five  pence a liter that only helps if you've  got a car a third of the population  don't have access to a car um  it it just seems to add to the to the  feeling that maybe you're not really in  touch with the hard reality of lots of  people's lives  actually when i go out about talking to  people fuel duty is one of the things  that is raised most often with me  because the vast majority of people in  this country do use their cars to get  around particularly those in rural areas  particularly those on lower incomes for  whom petrol bills are are a much larger  part of their income so this is  something actually that's reviewed we've  got a lovely picture of what happened  if i could just finish if i could just  finish answer the finish answering the  question and and actually talking to the  person who was doing that with it you  know they use their car every day to get  to work they notice obviously petrol  prices going up you know they were  appreciative of the fact that we're  cutting fuel duty it does demonstrate  we're on the side of people accepting  that we can't solve every problem or  fully fully make up for some of the  increase in petrol prices but with  regard to low earners and you mentioned  one thing tank you know that  also 