ng a conversation  about filling up you were just you were  just like about a quarter of a tank  well that's what was required but you i  mean one second ago you were saying that  all fuel doing something on fuel duty  isn't particularly valuable and and now  we're having conversation saying that it  is i think actually it is it's the  biggest ever i'm not saying that pure  duty isn't valuable  and i think and i think it will it will  make a difference to people i mean i  might you know my wife and i have  volkswagen golf and actually she's  filled it up as they're saying the other  week and and it is it is going up of  course we know it's going up and that's  why we took action uh yesterday to try  and make a difference there with the  biggest ever cut in fuel duty that we've  seen at five pennsylvania  and i'm really glad actually to see  retailers passing that on very quickly  and you know i hope that that happens  across the industry something i called  for yesterday and i'd see i think asda  and sainsbury's in particular were quick  out of the block saying that they were  doing that and i'm i'm grateful to them  for doing that so that your viewers we  can all benefit from that fuel duty cut  as quickly as possible  but there are those who who will will  will look at you and say that that you  aren't really in touch with real  people's lives i mean you've had a very  lucky and privileged life and i know  you've talked about the sacrifices your  parents made to send you to an excellent  school um but  i would suggest that you've never had to  worry about making a fiver last till the  end of the week  and there are a lot of people who who  are looking at what was announced  yesterday and we're talking to one of  them just after eight clark about a man  called barry who's a carer for for two  children who have got health issues his  wife's a care worker  and there was nothing there yesterday  they say for them that will make their  lives any easier and they're looking at  the with horror at the idea that  inflation could go up to as much as 10  percent  well you know this has been put to me  many times and what i say is judge me by  my actions i think people can see how  i've acted over the last couple of years  as our country went through a very  difficult period and i think i  demonstrated that i am standing by  british people when we go through  difficult things i introduce things like  furlough i help this country get through  a pandemic and partly because of the  actions that i took the economy is in a  much stronger place than anyone expected  now with more people on payroll than we  have before the pandemic unemployment  back down to the record low levels we  saw before the crisis that doesn't  happen by accident that happens because  of the policies that we put in place and  it's because of that that we can now  help people to whether some of the  challenges ahead accepting of course as  i've said we can't solve every problem i  wish i could and that's the toughest  part of this job not being able to do  everything that people want you to do  but where i can make a difference of  course that's what i'm doing and again  you know you talk about barry and his  family obviously you can't get into  everyone's individual circumstances but  i i do think families will benefit from  the fuel duty cut we announced yesterday  because many people are reliant on their  cars that will help  millions of people tens of millions of  people will benefit from the increase in  national insurance thresholds that's  going to save people 330 pounds a year  they'll benefit from the increase in the  national living wage they'll benefit  from the increased generosity of the  universal credit taper they'll benefit  from the extra money that we're giving  councils to help those most in need and  they will benefit from the income tax  cut that's coming down the line so look  there's a range of things we're doing  all of which demonstrate we are on the  side of hard-working british families  and that's what we will continue to do  how'd you say well the prime minister  has just uh made a statement apparently  he said we can do more to stop putin  from using his gold reserves what should  we be doing  i'd say no i i'm not sure the context of  exactly what the prime minister has said  that i'm talking to you but what i would  say is you know he's taken a lead and  deserves enormous credit for galvanizing  international opinion to deliver a  strong message he must have asked your  opinion as a chancellor  about the gold reserves well  yeah well there's lots of things we're  doing about the central bank and i've  seen that's what he's what he's talking  about and we've taken a leading role in  sanctioning russia particularly economic  and financial sanctions i outlined those  yesterday in parliament they are having  an impact and people should feel uh  reassured that what we are doing is  having an impact the russian ruble has  plummeted to record lows the moscow  stock exchange has been essentially  closed for a month and the russian  central bank has had to double interest  rates to 20 in russia so all the various  things we're doing  are having an impact and as the prime  minister said we will continue to  maintain pressure on russia  you you mentioned that you mentioned the  pressure on russia um  it's been reported uh that you've got  family links to russia that your wife  apparently has a stake in the indian i.t  consultancy firm infosys  they operate in moscow they have an  office there they have a delivery office  there they've got a connection to the  alpha bank in moscow  are you giving advice to others that  you're you're not following in your own  home  that's not as i i'm an elected  politician and i'm here to talk to you  about what i'm responsible for my wife  is not  she is not but but equally if you you  know as a country we are asking  taxpayers to fund the uk's support for  ukraine we're asking people in the uk to  give their homes up to ukrainian  refugees  whereas it appears your family  potentially could be benefiting from  putin's regime  no i i really i don't think that's the  case and as i said the the operations of  all companies are up to them uh we've  we've put in place significant sanctions  and all the companies that we're  responsible for are following those as  they rightly should sending a very  strong message uh to putin's aggression  do you know if infosys is  i i have i have absolutely no idea  because i have nothing to do with that  company  okay  um  in terms of of what the prime minister  is saying he is saying that we could be  doing more to stop putin what would you  like to see the british government doing  more  to stop putin and and where will that  money come from we've talked about a new  support package for ukrainian forces 6  000 more anti-tank missiles is this  going to come from our existing defense  budget  also we we knew that russia was a  particular threat to our security uh  because in the something called the  integrated review which was done a  little while ago it was named very  specifically is a big threat and that's  why we increased our defense budget by  record amounts 24 billion pounds over  the four year period that's the biggest  increase in defense spending since the  end of the cold war in reflection of the  new threats that we face and it's  because of that extra investment in  defense that we're able to provide  support to our allies like ukraine at  this difficult time and we know that  that is making a difference to their  ability to withstand russian aggression  and i hope people are proud of the  contribution that the british state and  armed forces are playing  chancellor rishi sunak thank you very  much for joining us so we appreciate  your time  thank you

